<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Ulster Med J</journal-id><journal-title>The Ulster Medical Journal</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0041-6193</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">6531831</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2447948</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">6531831</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Articles</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Towards Painless Orthopaedic Surgery</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Barron</surname><given-names>David W.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>1984</year></pub-date><volume>53</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>146</fpage><lpage>149</lpage><abstract><p>Almost one thousand patients underwent either total hip replacement or spinal surgery in which diamorphine 0.5-1.0 mg was injected intrathecally. Approximately one half of the arthroplasty patients and one third of the spinal patients required no other post-operative analgesia. In the remainder, analgesics were not required for 12 hours post-operatively. The benefits of the technique and possible complications are discussed.</p></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="ulstermedj00094-0049.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="146" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="ulstermedj00094-0050.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="147" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="ulstermedj00094-0051.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="148" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="ulstermedj00094-0052.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="149" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>